<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indo-European languages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indo-European languages are a family of related languages spoken across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and much of western and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, descended from a reconstructed ancestor, Proto-Indo-European, probably spoken in the 4th millennium BCE. Its branches include Germanic, Romance, Celtic, Slavic, Greek, Armenian, Iranian and Indo-Aryan, together with extinct groups such as Anatolian (Hittite) and Tocharian. The family was identified in the late 18th and 19th centuries by comparing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages.jpg" alt="Indo-European languages" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Bill Williams · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indo-European_Language_Family_Branches_in_Eurasia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
